**TECHNOLOGY BRIEF: Implementation of Cross-System Java Echo Client-Server Architecture**

**OVERVIEW**
In the field of network programming, developers frequently utilize socket programming to establish reliable communication between distinct computing systems. A standard baseline for testing these network connections is an "echo" client-server model. In this architecture, a client system transmits a text string to a server system over a network; the server processes the incoming data and returns the exact string to the client, confirming bilateral connectivity. 

Below is an objective, functional overview of how this communication is established using the Java programming language across two different systems on the same local area network (LAN).

***

### 1. The Server Program (System A)
System A acts as the host. It opens a specific network port (in this case, port 5000) and listens for incoming connection requests. 

**Filename:** `EchoServer.java`
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class EchoServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int port = 5000;

        try (ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(port)) {
            System.out.println("Server started on port " + port + ". Waiting for client connection...");

            // Server pauses here until a client connects
            Socket clientSocket = serverSocket.accept();
            System.out.println("Client connected from IP: " + clientSocket.getInetAddress().getHostAddress());

            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(clientSocket.getOutputStream(), true);
            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(clientSocket.getInputStream()));

            String inputLine;
            // Read messages from the client and echo them back
            while ((inputLine = in.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println("Received from client: " + inputLine);
                out.println("Server Echo: " + inputLine);

                if ("exit".equalsIgnoreCase(inputLine)) {
                    break;
                }
            }
            System.out.println("Server connection closed.");
        } catch (IOException e) {
