Unity’s built-in `[Header("...")]` attribute does not natively support collapsing. However, there are three common ways to achieve collapsible sections, ranging from zero-dependency tricks to using popular free plugins.

---

### Method 1: Use Nested Classes/Structs (No Plugins Required)
Unity automatically makes any `[System.Serializable]` class or struct collapsible in the Inspector. This is the cleanest built-in way to organize fields without writing custom editor code.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [System.Serializable]
    public class MovementSettings
    {
        public float moveSpeed = 5f;
        public float jumpForce = 10f;
    }

    [System.Serializable]
    public class CombatSettings
    {
        public int health = 100;
        public float attackCooldown = 1.2f;
    }

    // These will appear as collapsible dropdowns in the Inspector
    public MovementSettings movement;
    public CombatSettings combat;
}
```

---

### Method 2: Use **NaughtyAttributes** (Free & Recommended)
If you do not want to nest variables inside classes, the free open-source package **[NaughtyAttributes](https://github.com/dbrizov/NaughtyAttributes)**

Here is a complete C++ program using the **OpenCV** library to detect faces in real-time from a webcam feed using a pre-trained Haar Cascade classifier.

### Prerequisites

1. **OpenCV** installed on your system.
2. The pre-trained Haar Cascade XML file for frontal face detection (`haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml`). You can find this in your OpenCV installation directory (typically under `data/haarcascades/`) or download it from OpenCV's official GitHub repository. Place it in the same directory as your executable.

---

### C++ Code (`face_detect.cpp`)

```cpp
#include <opencv2/opencv.hpp>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // 1. Load the Haar Cascade classifier for face detection
    std::string cascade_path = "haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml";
    cv::CascadeClassifier face_cascade;

    if (!face_cascade.load(cascade_path)) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Could not load classifier XML file: " 
                  << cascade_path << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // 2. Open the default camera (ID 0)
    cv::VideoCapture capture(0);
    if (!capture.isOpened()) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Could not access the webcam." << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    cv::Mat frame, gray_frame;
    std::cout << "Starting face detection. Press 'ESC' to exit." << std::endl;

    while (true) {
        // Capture frame-by-frame
        capture >> frame;
        if (frame.empty()) {
            std::cerr << "Warning: Empty frame received." << std::endl;
            break;
        }

        // Convert frame to grayscale (Haar Cascades work on grayscale images)
        cv::cvtColor(frame, gray_frame, cv::COLOR_BGR2GRAY);
        // Equalize histogram to improve contrast and detection accuracy
        cv::equalizeHist(gray_frame, gray_frame);

        // Vector to store detected face boundaries
        std::vector<cv::Rect> faces;

