Here's an example of how you can create a simple HTML client using the `librejs` library and `request` library in Node.js. This client will make a GET request to a URL, retrieve the HTML response, and then execute the HTML code using `librejs`.

**Note:** This is a basic example and may not cover all edge cases. You may need to modify and extend this code to suit your specific requirements.

**Client.js**
```javascript
const request = require('request');
const librejs = require('librejs');

class HTMLClient {
  constructor(url) {
    this.url = url;
  }

  async getHtml() {
    try {
      const response = await request(this.url);
      const html = response.body;
      return html;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      return null;
    }
  }

  executeHtml(html) {
    librejs.execute(html, (err, document) => {
      if (err) {
        console.error(err);
      } else {
        console.log(document);
      }
    });
  }
}

module.exports = HTMLClient;
```
**index.js**
```javascript
const HTMLClient = require('./Client');

const client = new HTMLClient('https://example.com');

client.getHtml().then((html) => {
  if (html) {
    client.executeHtml(html);
  }
}).catch((error) => {
  console.error(error);
});
```
**Explanation:**

1. We create an `HTMLClient` class that takes a URL in its constructor.
2. The `getHtml` method makes a GET request to the specified URL using `request`.
3. The `executeHtml` method uses `librejs` to execute the HTML code. It takes the HTML response as an argument and logs the resulting document to the console.
4. In the `index.js` file, we create an instance of the `HTMLClient` class and call the `getHtml` method to retrieve the HTML response.
5. If the HTML response is successful, we call the `executeHtml` method to execute the HTML code.

**Note:** This example assumes that you have `librejs` and `request` installed as dependencies. You may need to modify the code to fit your specific requirements.

**Example Use Case:**

To use this code, simply replace the `https://example.com` URL with the URL of the HTML page you want to execute. The client will retrieve the HTML response and execute the HTML code using `librejs`.

